So the equivalent in C would be:<p><pre><code>        $ cat main.c
        #include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
        #include &lt;string.h&gt;

        struct Q {
                &#x2F;*@null@*&#x2F; &#x2F;*@only@*&#x2F; char * t;
        };


        &#x2F;*@only@*&#x2F;
        struct Q *
        Q_new (&#x2F;*@only@*&#x2F; char * t)
        {
                struct Q * q = malloc (sizeof *q);
                q-&gt;t = t;
                return q;
        }

        void
        Q_set (struct Q * q, &#x2F;*@only@*&#x2F; char * t)
        {
                free (q-&gt;t);
                q-&gt;t = t;
                return;
        }



        int
        main (void)
        {
                &#x2F;*@only@*&#x2F; char * l;
                &#x2F;*@only@*&#x2F; struct Q * q;

                l = strdup (&quot;&quot;);
                q = Q_new (NULL);

                Q_set (q, l);
                free (l);

                return EXIT_SUCCESS;
        }

        $ splint main.c
        Splint 3.1.2 --- 21 Feb 2021

        main.c: (in function Q_new)
        main.c:15:3: Arrow access from possibly null pointer q: q-&gt;t
          A possibly null pointer is dereferenced.  Value is either the result of a
          function which may return null (in which case, code should check it is not
          null), or a global, parameter or structure field declared with the null
          qualifier. (Use -nullderef to inhibit warning)
           main.c:14:17: Storage q may become null
        main.c: (in function main)
        main.c:36:13: Null storage passed as non-null param: Q_new (NULL)
          A possibly null pointer is passed as a parameter corresponding to a formal
          parameter with no &#x2F;*@null@*&#x2F; annotation.  If NULL may be used for this
          parameter, add a &#x2F;*@null@*&#x2F; annotation to the function parameter declaration.
          (Use -nullpass to inhibit warning)
        main.c:39:8: Dead storage l passed as out parameter to free: l
          Memory is used after it has been released (either by passing as an only param
          or assigning to an only global). (Use -usereleased to inhibit warning)
           main.c:38:12: Storage l released
        main.c:41:22: Fresh storage q not released before return
          A memory leak has been detected. Storage allocated locally is not released
          before the last reference to it is lost. (Use -mustfreefresh to inhibit
          warning)
           main.c:36:2: Fresh storage q created
        main.c:12:1: Function exported but not used outside main: Q_new
          A declaration is exported, but not used outside this module. Declaration can
          use static qualifier. (Use -exportlocal to inhibit warning)
           main.c:17:1: Definition of Q_new
        main.c:20:1: Function exported but not used outside main: Q_set
           main.c:25:1: Definition of Q_set

        Finished checking --- 6 code warnings
</code></pre>
(The equivalent warning is main.c:39)

So Q is a &quot;tuple-struct&quot;. In rust you can define a struct like<p>struct Q&lt;T&gt;  {
  value: Option&lt;T&gt;
}<p>with a named field or you can define it like<p>struct&lt;T&gt; Q(Some&lt;T&gt;)<p>which is the same struct layout but without an explicit name for the value. In that case, the field is implicitly names `0`<p>So q.0 = Some(foo) is equivalent to q.value = Some(foo).<p>The Some(..) is just one variant of the Option enum:<p>enum Option&lt;T&gt; {
  None,
  Some(T)
}<p>Option represents a value that may or may not be initialized. For uninitialized values you use the None variant and then you can initialize it to Some(value).<p>The C rough equivalent would be<p>struct Q {
  void* value;
}<p>struct Q q = q { NULL };
q-&gt;value = ...

&quot;Some(...)&quot; means that this Option&lt;T&gt; does have a value of type T in it; &quot;None&quot; means it doesn&#x27;t.<p>&quot;q.0&quot; is not really needed, it&#x27;s a struct with unnamed fields. Here is the same with named fields:<p><pre><code>  struct Q&lt;T: &#x27;static&gt; { pub x: Option&lt;T&gt; }
  ...
  let l = String::new();
  let mut q = Q { x: None };
  q.x = Some(&amp;l);</code></pre>

